        "abstract": "First I wish to comment on Dr. A. E. H. Bleksley's discussion. Dr. Bleksley mentions that in the low-pressure lamps a good deal of energy is wasted due to inappropriate excitation. As I briefly remarked in connection with sodium lamps, the presence of a large number of neon atoms (8--10,000 times as many as sodium atoms) has a second role (besides being the starting medium) of allowing further sodium excitation by subsequent collisions. This partnership of neon having a substantially higher pressure than the sodium gas itself, results in reducing the length of the average free path in the discharge. This regenerative temperature, it may be assumed that, in general, modern high-voltage condenser bushings should comply with curve Q. It might be of interest to record that we have carried out tests to determine the change in power-factor with temperature of the insulation of a metal-clad 22 kV oil circuit-breaker with condenser insulators and compound filled hoods and complete with oil. The entire switch was put in an oven and readings taken up to 90° the results are given in Fig. A.",
